    </div>

    <p>
        Every platformer ever shipped uses one of these four. Read all of them — the differences
        are in what each one can <em>tell you</em> and where each one lies to you. The walkthrough
        below builds the first row, because that is the check 7.2 expects you to own.
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Technique</th><th>The question you are asking</th><th>What it needs</th><th>Where it lies to you</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Physics2D.Raycast(origin, Vector2.down, distance, mask)</code></td>
            <td>"Is there a ground-layer collider directly below this exact point?"</td>
            <td>An origin (your feet, not your centre), a distance and a <strong>LayerMask</strong>.</td>
            <td>One infinitely thin line: stand with a pixel of your foot over the edge of a tile and it reports <em>air</em>. It returns a <code>RaycastHit2D</code> — collider, distance and contact point — so it is also the only row that can tell you <em>how far</em>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Physics2D.OverlapCircle(point, radius, mask)</code></td>
            <td>"Is there a ground-layer collider anywhere inside this circle at my feet?"</td>
            <td>A point, a radius and a LayerMask.</td>
            <td>A wall beside your feet is inside the circle too, so you can be "grounded" against a vertical surface. It returns the collider only — no direction, no distance.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Collider2D.IsTouchingLayers(mask)</code></td>
            <td>"Is my own collider touching anything on this layer, right now?"</td>
            <td>Your Collider2D and a LayerMask — no origin, no distance, no ray.</td>
            <td>It returns a single true or false, and only sees contacts that exist: a <strong>Kinematic</strong> body reports false while standing on the floor unless <em>Use Full Kinematic Contacts</em> is ticked.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>OnCollisionStay2D(Collision2D c)</code> with a layer check</td>
            <td>"What am I in contact with during this physics step?"</td>
            <td>A callback method, plus a rule to stop it re-firing your jump every step.</td>
            <td>It fires continuously, so a naive jump fires continuously. And for a Kinematic body against static colliders it gives you the <em>message</em> without the collision — hearing a floor is never the same as standing on one.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
${renderWalkthrough(WALK)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: Prove It, Then Break It</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 1 — the honesty test.</strong> Press Play with the Player selected so the gizmo
        draws, and watch it through four actions: stand still (<em>true</em>), jump (<em>false</em>
        the moment you leave the ground), land (<em>true</em>), walk off a ledge (<em>false</em>).
        If any is wrong, your <strong>distance</strong> or your <strong>origin</strong> is wrong —
        not your logic. Move the empty child or change the number, and run the four again.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 2 — the mask experiment.</strong> Set your mask to <strong>Everything</strong>
        and press Play: if your origin sits inside your own collider the ray hits <em>you</em> at
        distance zero and reports ground forever — the jetpack is back, wearing a disguise. Put it
        back to <code>Ground</code>, then set it to <strong>Nothing</strong> and watch the opposite
        failure: a character who cannot jump at all, silently.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 3 — tune by measurement.</strong> Read the real gap from the bottom of your
        collider to the tile surface in the Scene view and set the number from that. Then halve it,
        play, and watch the jump start failing on ground you can see. Guessing produces exactly
        that failure.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 4 — walk the whole cave with the gizmo on.</strong> Every place the line is red
        while your feet are on something solid is a surface missing from your <code>Ground</code>
        layer — and there is at least one, because this cave has two solid tilemaps.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: the check.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>Which technique did you choose, and what are its exact inputs — origin, direction, distance, mask? If you chose anything other than the raycast the walkthrough built, map those four inputs onto yours.</li>
            <li>Where does the origin sit, and how did you <em>find out</em> rather than assume? The Player root is not the centre of the character you can see.</li>
            <li>Where does the mask come from — a serialized <code>LayerMask</code> field, or a hard-coded layer name? Which one lets a teammate fix your level without opening your script?</li>
            <li>Is the jump condition now compound? Write the whole sentence in English before you write it in C#.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: making the invisible visible.</strong> Before you trust
        a check you cannot see, draw it: <code>OnDrawGizmosSelected()</code> runs in the editor and
        can draw a line from your origin along your check direction, scaled by your distance, in one
        colour when the check is false and another when it is true. Drawing it costs your game
        nothing, because nothing in a gizmo method ships.
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Explain the difference between a tag and a layer, using two solid tilemaps from your own cave as the example</li>
        <li>Create a layer and assign it to every surface you can land on — and say why the cave mouth needs it even though nobody tagged it</li>
        <li>Compare the four ground-check techniques and defend your choice</li>
        <li>Show the check with a gizmo and tune the distance by measurement, not by guessing</li>
        <li>Explain the self-hit bug and the Nothing mask, and why a LayerMask stands between you and both</li>
        <li>Explain why the grounded value is re-derived every frame instead of stored when you land</li>
        <li>Commit and push</li>
    </ul>
</div>

